who planned to do a mass shooting and change their mind. And when they changed their mind, and some of these were even men who were at the school with the gun in their backpack and changed their mind. and it tends to be a human connection, right? Somebody connecting with them, somebody getting him through the moment, somebody giving them a little bit of hope, somebody seeing them. One in particular, this man is a TED talk, Aaron Stark, but he talks about how he was planning on doing a mass shooting.
他们曾计划实施大规模枪击但改变了主意。当他们改变主意时——其中一些人甚至是在学校里、背包里藏着枪但改变了主意的男性——这往往归结于人类的某种连接，对吧？有人与他们建立连接，有人陪他们度过那个时刻，有人给予他们一丝希望，有人看见了他们。其中特别有一个人，这个人做过TED演讲，叫亚伦·斯塔克（Aaron Stark），他谈到自己曾计划实施大规模枪击。

40
00:26:28.000 --> 00:27:02.000
And kind of the day before, he went over his friend's house, and his friend's mom had baked a pie because he was coming over. And sitting down and eating that pie, was, it stopped him from doing a mass shooting. Like it was that powerful of a moment that somebody would have thought, I'm going to make a pie in his honor. It was just a human connection. And so sometimes I think we can get so caught at what we need to do societally that we sometimes forget that we can all just be baking pies for each other. You never know when that connection is actually having a really meaningful moment for somebody.
就在前一天左右，他去了朋友家，朋友的妈妈因为他要来烤了一个馅饼。坐下来吃那个馅饼，就是这件事，阻止了他去实施大规模枪击。这是一种如此强大的时刻，本以为会有人想：“我要为了他的荣誉烤个馅饼。”这只是一种人与人之间的连接。因此，有时我认为我们太纠结于我们在社会层面上需要做什么，以至于有时忘记了我们其实都可以为彼此烤馅饼。你永远不知道这种连接什么时候会真正对某人产生非常有意义的影响。

